{"toolCallId":"call_5a66de7d030b43eab0a7f14efc0591fd","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Task Format: T### [P?] Description (file path)\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag tasks\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.3 -->\n# Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n## Task Notation\n<!-- FILL: Keep the prefix table and add the two shell variables this task list uses. GOOD: `S` is the script path. `P` is the packet path. WEAK: Variables are defined below. -->\n\n| Prefix | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `[ ]` | Pending |\n| `[x]` | Completed |\n| `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n| `[B]` | Blocked |\n\n**Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n<!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n## Phase 1: Setup\n<!-- FILL: Replace the sample tasks with the real setup steps. Each task names a file and a verify command. GOOD: T001 Read the output block in `recommend-level.sh`. Verify: `rg -n 'Recommended Level'` prints one line. WEAK: T001 Set up the project. -->\n\n- [ ] T001 Create project structure\n- [ ] T002 Install dependencies\n- [ ] T003 [P] Configure development tools\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n## Phase 2: Implementation\n<!-- FILL: Write one implementation task per file change. Add a `Verify:` line with the command and its expected output. GOOD: T002 Add the `--quiet` branch. Verify: `--quiet` prints one line. WEAK: T002 Implement the feature. -->\n\n- [ ] T004 [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Implement core feature 1]\n- [ ] T005 [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Implement core feature 2]\n- [ ] T006 [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Implement core feature 3]\n- [ ] T007 [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Add error handling]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n## Phase 3: Verification\n<!-- FILL: Write one verification task per requirement. Each one runs a command. GOOD: T003 Run both invocations. Verify: `wc -l` prints 1 and 17. WEAK: T003 Test everything. -->\n\n- [ ] T008 Test happy path manually\n- [ ] T009 Test edge cases\n- [ ] T010 Update documentation\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n## Completion Criteria\n<!-- FILL: Keep the three completion lines and add the one artifact that proves the phase is done. GOOD: `--quiet` prints one line and the suite passes. WEAK: Work is complete. -->\n\n- [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n- [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n- [ ] Manual verification passed\n<!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n## Cross-References\n<!-- FILL: Name the spec and plan files. Add the decision record when the packet has one. GOOD: Specification: `spec.md`. Plan: `plan.md`. WEAK: See the other docs. -->\n\n- **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n- **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n<!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n\n---\n\n## Verification Checklist\n<!-- FILL: Fill each checklist section below. -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n## Verification Protocol\n<!-- FILL: Keep the three rows and add the rule this packet uses for a blocked task. GOOD: P0 blocks close. P1 needs a recorded reason. WEAK: Follow the priorities. -->\n\n| Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n|----------|----------|-------------------|\n| **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n| **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n| **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n## Pre-Implementation\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per setup fact, each with a command. GOOD: CHK-001 [P0] The start commit is in `goal.md` section 4. WEAK: Setup is ready. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-001 [P0] Requirements documented in spec.md\n- [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Technical approach defined in plan.md\n- [ ] CHK-003 [P1] Dependencies identified and available\n<!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n## Code Quality\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per file rule. A command must answer it. GOOD: CHK-010 [P0] `bash -n recommend-level.sh` exits 0. WEAK: Code is clean. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-010 [P0] Code passes lint/format checks\n- [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No console errors or warnings\n- [ ] CHK-012 [P1] Error handling implemented\n- [ ] CHK-013 [P1] Code follows project patterns\n<!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## Testing Checklist\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per acceptance criterion. GOOD: CHK-020 [P0] `--quiet` prints one line. WEAK: Tests pass. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-020 [P0] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] CHK-021 [P0] Manual testing complete\n- [ ] CHK-022 [P1] Edge cases tested\n- [ ] CHK-023 [P1] Error scenarios validated\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n## Fix Completeness\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per finding class this packet can produce. Delete the rows that do not apply. GOOD: CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Every finding names one class. WEAK: All findings handled. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Each actionable finding has a finding class: `instance-only`, `class-of-bug`, `cross-consumer`, `algorithmic`, `matrix/evidence`, or `test-isolation`.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producer inventory completed, or instance-only status proven by grep.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Consumer inventory completed for changed helpers, policies, schema fields, response fields, docs, and tests.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P0] Security/path/parser/redaction fixes include adversarial table tests for delimiter, joined-input, outside-root, no-op, and fallback cases.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] Matrix axes and row count are listed before completion is claimed.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] Hostile env/global-state variant executed when tests or code read process-wide state.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-007 [P1] Evidence is pinned to a fix SHA or explicit diff range, not a moving branch-relative range.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n## Security\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per security surface. Write `N/A` with a reason when none applies. GOOD: CHK-030 [P0] The script reads no credential. WEAK: Security is fine. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No hardcoded secrets\n- [ ] CHK-031 [P0] Input validation implemented\n- [ ] CHK-032 [P1] Auth/authz working correctly\n<!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n## Documentation\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per document that must agree. GOOD: CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md` and `tasks.md` name the same flag. WEAK: Docs are updated. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-040 [P1] Spec/plan/tasks synchronized\n- [ ] CHK-041 [P1] Code comments adequate\n- [ ] CHK-042 [P2] README updated (if applicable)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n## File Organization\n<!-- FILL: Write one check per file location rule. GOOD: CHK-050 [P1] Test copies live under `scratch/test/`. WEAK: Files are organized. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Temp files in scratch/ only\n- [ ] CHK-051 [P1] scratch/ cleaned before completion\n<!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## Verification Summary\n<!-- FILL: Count the boxes by priority and write the three totals. GOOD: P0 12, P1 13, P2 1. WEAK: Most items done. -->\n\n| Category | Total | Verified |\n|----------|-------|----------|\n| P0 Items | [X] | [ ]/[X] |\n| P1 Items | [Y] | [ ]/[Y] |\n| P2 Items | [Z] | [ ]/[Z] |\n\n**Verification Date**: 2026-10-25\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n## L3+: Architecture Verification\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Write one check per ADR, or delete the section when the packet records no ADR. GOOD: CHK-100 [P0] ADR-001 is followed by T002. WEAK: Architecture is verified. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-100 [P0] Architecture decisions documented in decision-record.md\n- [ ] CHK-101 [P1] All ADRs have status (Proposed/Accepted)\n- [ ] CHK-102 [P1] Alternatives documented with rejection rationale\n- [ ] CHK-103 [P2] Migration path documented (if applicable)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n## L3+: Performance Verification\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Write one check per performance claim, or delete the section when the packet makes none. GOOD: CHK-110 [P1] The flag adds under 5 ms. WEAK: Performance is verified. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-110 [P1] Response time targets met (NFR-P01)\n- [ ] CHK-111 [P1] Throughput targets met (NFR-P02)\n- [ ] CHK-112 [P2] Load testing completed\n- [ ] CHK-113 [P2] Performance benchmarks documented\n<!-- /ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n## L3+: Deployment Readiness\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Write one check per deployment step, or delete the section when the packet ships no service. GOOD: CHK-120 [P0] The rollback command was run in a copy. WEAK: Ready to deploy. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-120 [P0] Rollback procedure documented and tested\n- [ ] CHK-121 [P0] Feature flag configured (if applicable)\n- [ ] CHK-122 [P1] Monitoring/alerting configured\n- [ ] CHK-123 [P1] Runbook created\n- [ ] CHK-124 [P2] Deployment runbook reviewed\n<!-- /ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:compliance-verify -->\n## L3+: Compliance Verification\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Write one check per compliance surface, or delete the section when none applies. GOOD: CHK-130 [P1] No license changed. WEAK: Compliance is verified. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-130 [P1] Security review completed\n- [ ] CHK-131 [P1] Dependency licenses compatible\n- [ ] CHK-132 [P2] OWASP Top 10 checklist completed\n- [ ] CHK-133 [P2] Data handling compliant with requirements\n<!-- /ANCHOR:compliance-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:docs-verify -->\n## L3+: Documentation Verification\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Write one check per document pair that must agree, or delete the section when the packet holds no paired documents. GOOD: CHK-140 [P1] `plan.md` and `tasks.md` agree on the three phases. WEAK: Docs are verified. -->\n\n- [ ] CHK-140 [P1] All spec documents synchronized\n- [ ] CHK-141 [P1] API documentation complete (if applicable)\n- [ ] CHK-142 [P2] User-facing documentation updated\n- [ ] CHK-143 [P2] Knowledge transfer documented\n<!-- /ANCHOR:docs-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:sign-off -->\n## L3+: Sign-Off\n<!-- FILL: L3+ means Level 3 and above, so this section stays at Level 3. Name the real approver for each role. Delete a row when the role does not exist. Write `N/A` or delete the section when no sign-off applies. GOOD: Conductor, runs every task. Operator, merges the commit. WEAK: Signed off. -->\n\n| Approver | Role | Status | Date |\n|----------|------|--------|------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Name] | Technical Lead | [ ] Approved | |\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Name] | Product Owner | [ ] Approved | |\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Name] | QA Lead | [ ] Approved | |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:sign-off -->\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Task Format: T### [P?] Description (file path)\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag tasks\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.3 -->\n# Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n## Task Notation\n\n| Prefix | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `[ ]` | Pending |\n| `[x]` | Completed |\n| `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n| `[B]` | Blocked |\n\n**Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n\nVariables used below: `S` = `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`, `P` = `specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n## Phase 1: Setup\n\n- [ ] T001 Read the argument-parse loop, `output_text()`, `output_json()`, `show_help()` and the output dispatch in `$S`. Verify: `rg -n 'output_text|output_json|show_help|JSON_OUTPUT|Recommended Level' $S` prints each site.\n- [ ] T002 Capture baseline output for the verification-matrix invocations into `$P/scratch/baseline/` — the default report, `--json`, `--no-recommend-phases`, `--help`, and each error case. Verify: `ls $P/scratch/baseline/` lists one file per invocation.\n- [ ] T003 [P] Inventory the producers and consumers of the level line. Verify: `rg -n 'Recommended Level' $S` prints one site today, and `rg -ln 'recommend-level' .skilled --glob '*.sh'` finds no caller that parses the report.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n## Phase 2: Implementation\n\n- [ ] T004 Add `QUIET=false` to the globals and a `--quiet)` case to the parse loop in `$S`. Verify: `bash $S --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 >/dev/null && echo OK` prints `OK`, and the same command with `--quiet` placed last also exits 0.\n- [ ] T005 Add `output_level_line()` to the output-helper section of `$S` and replace the level literal inside `output_text()` with a call to it. Verify: `rg -n 'output_level_line|Recommended Level' $S` prints the definition, the report call and one literal.\n- [ ] T006 Add the quiet branch to the output dispatch in `$S`, after the `--json` check and before `output_text`. Verify: `bash $S --quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db` prints exactly `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and `| wc -l` prints `1`.\n- [ ] T007 Document `--quiet` in the header OPTIONS comment and in `show_help()` in `$S`. Verify: `bash $S --help | grep -c -- '--quiet'` prints `1` or more.\n- [ ] T008 [P] Confirm the required-input validation still runs before output selection. Verify: `bash $S --quiet` exits 1, writes the missing-input error to stderr, and leaves stdout empty.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n## Phase 3: Verification\n\n- [ ] T009 Run the full flag matrix: default, `--quiet`, `--json`, `--quiet --json`, quiet with `--recommend-phases`, `--no-recommend-phases` and `--phase-threshold`, `--quiet --help`, and each error case under `--quiet`. Verify: line counts, the `Recommended Level:` line and exit codes match `spec.md` §5.\n- [ ] T010 Byte-diff the post-change default and `--json` outputs against `$P/scratch/baseline/`. Verify: `diff` prints nothing for every non-quiet invocation.\n- [ ] T011 Compare the quiet line with the report's level line for the same inputs. Verify: `diff <(bash $S --quiet --loc 100 --files 3) <(bash $S --loc 100 --files 3 | sed -n '2p')` prints nothing.\n- [ ] T012 Sync the packet docs with what was built and run `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh $P --strict`. Verify: the run prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n## Completion Criteria\n\n- [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n- [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n- [ ] Manual verification passed — the quiet run prints one line, every non-quiet `diff` is empty, and strict validation prints `RESULT: PASSED`\n<!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n## Cross-References\n\n- **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n- **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Decision Record**: ADR-001 in `plan.md` §L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n<!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n\n---\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n## Verification Protocol\n\n| Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n|----------|----------|-------------------|\n| **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n| **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n| **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n\nBlocked-task rule for this packet: a `[B]` task blocks closure until it is retried or its blocker is recorded beside it with the failing command's output.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n## Pre-Implementation\n\n- [ ] CHK-001 [P0] Requirements documented in spec.md — REQ-001 to REQ-009 each name an observable output, exit code or command.\n- [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Technical approach defined in plan.md — parse case, shared helper, dispatch branch and help text.\n- [ ] CHK-003 [P1] Baseline captured: `ls $P/scratch/baseline/` lists one file per matrix invocation.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n## Code Quality\n\n- [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `bash -n $S` exits 0 after the edit.\n- [ ] CHK-011 [P0] Success paths write nothing to stderr: `bash $S --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 2>&1 >/dev/null` prints nothing.\n- [ ] CHK-012 [P1] Error paths are unchanged: missing inputs, non-numeric values and unknown options exit 1 with the same stderr text (REQ-005).\n- [ ] CHK-013 [P1] The quiet line reuses the report's format: `rg -n 'Recommended Level' $S` shows one literal, inside `output_level_line()` (REQ-003).\n<!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## Testing Checklist\n\n- [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every acceptance-criteria row verified with its named command (AC-001 to AC-009).\n- [ ] CHK-021 [P0] Manual matrix run complete: default, `--quiet`, `--json`, `--quiet --json`, quiet with phase flags and `--quiet --help` (T009).\n- [ ] CHK-022 [P1] Edge cases tested: empty input, extreme `--loc`/`--files`, and `--quiet` in different argument positions.\n- [ ] CHK-023 [P1] Error scenarios validated: missing arguments, non-numeric value and unknown flag — exit 1, stderr only.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n## Fix Completeness\n\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Same-class producer inventory complete: `rg -n 'Recommended Level' $S` confirms one format site that the quiet path shares.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Consumer inventory complete: `rg -ln 'recommend-level' .skilled --glob '*.sh' --glob '*.ts' --glob '*.cjs'` shows no production caller whose parsing could break.\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Contract preservation proven: the byte comparison of the default and `--json` output against the baseline is empty (T010).\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] Matrix axes and row count are listed before completion is claimed — the axes are in `plan.md` §FIX ADDENDUM and the rows in T009.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n## Security\n\n- [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No hardcoded secret is introduced — the diff adds a boolean flag and one helper.\n- [ ] CHK-031 [P0] `--quiet` takes no value, so it adds no input-validation surface; the existing numeric checks still run first.\n- [ ] CHK-032 [P1] N/A — the script performs no authentication or authorization, so there is nothing to verify.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n## Documentation\n\n- [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` describe the same semantics: the text path only, with `--json` unchanged.\n- [ ] CHK-041 [P1] Any comment added to `$S` states the durable why only — no requirement ids, task ids or spec paths.\n- [ ] CHK-042 [P2] Help surface updated: `bash $S --help | grep -n -- '--quiet'` prints the new option (REQ-007).\n<!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n## File Organization\n\n- [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Baseline captures and any scratch output live only under `$P/scratch/`.\n- [ ] CHK-051 [P1] `scratch/` is cleaned of throwaway output before completion is claimed; deliberate evidence is named as kept.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## Verification Summary\n\n| Category | Total | Verified |\n|----------|-------|----------|\n| P0 Items | 14 | 0/14 |\n| P1 Items | 21 | 0/21 |\n| P2 Items | 9 | 0/9 |\n\n**Verification Date**: 2026-10-25\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n## L3+: Architecture Verification\n\nThis section applies at Level 3 and above, so it is filled rather than removed.\n\n- [ ] CHK-100 [P0] ADR-001 is recorded in `plan.md` and followed by T005–T006: the text path shortens, `--json` is untouched.\n- [ ] CHK-101 [P1] ADR-001 carries a status (`Proposed`).\n- [ ] CHK-102 [P1] ADR-001 names the rejected alternatives — bare-value output, an environment variable and a `-q` alias — with a rationale for each.\n- [ ] CHK-103 [P2] N/A — an additive flag has no migration path.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n## L3+: Performance Verification\n\n- [ ] CHK-110 [P1] `--quiet` adds no measurable time: the scoring path is unchanged and only the output branch differs (NFR-P01).\n- [ ] CHK-111 [P1] N/A — the packet sets no throughput target; the script is single-shot.\n- [ ] CHK-112 [P2] N/A — no load test applies to a one-shot CLI.\n- [ ] CHK-113 [P2] N/A — there is no benchmark to document.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n## L3+: Deployment Readiness\n\n- [ ] CHK-120 [P0] The rollback procedure is documented (`plan.md` §7) and its proof step — revert the single-file diff and re-run T009 — is executable.\n- [ ] CHK-121 [P0] The change needs no feature flag: `--quiet` is itself opt-in and the default path is untouched.\n- [ ] CHK-122 [P1] N/A — no monitoring or alerting applies to a standalone CLI script.\n- [ ] CHK-123 [P1] N/A — `--help` is the runbook; nothing further is needed.\n- [ ] CHK-124 [P2] N/A — there is no deployment runbook to review.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:compliance-verify -->\n## L3+: Compliance Verification\n\n- [ ] CHK-130 [P1] Security review complete: no new input surface, no new file access and no new command execution (CHK-030, CHK-031).\n- [ ] CHK-131 [P1] No dependency was added, so no license compatibility question exists.\n- [ ] CHK-132 [P2] N/A — the OWASP Top 10 does not apply to a local boolean flag.\n- [ ] CHK-133 [P2] N/A — the script handles no user data.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:compliance-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:docs-verify -->\n## L3+: Documentation Verification\n\n- [ ] CHK-140 [P1] All packet documents agree: `spec.md` REQ-001 to REQ-009 map one-to-one to `acceptance-criteria.md` AC-001 to AC-009 and to the checks here.\n- [ ] CHK-141 [P1] N/A — a shell flag has no API documentation.\n- [ ] CHK-142 [P2] The user-facing surface is updated: `show_help()` and the header OPTIONS comment list `--quiet`.\n- [ ] CHK-143 [P2] Knowledge transfer: this packet's spec, plan and tasks are the record; no separate handoff is needed.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:docs-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:sign-off -->\n## L3+: Sign-Off\n\n| Approver | Role | Status | Date |\n|----------|------|--------|------|\n| Implementer | Self-verification through this checklist and the T009–T012 commands | [ ] Approved | |\n| Reviewer | Packet review before closure | [ ] Approved | |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:sign-off -->\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n- [ ] Read `recommend-level.sh` before editing — understand the parse loop, the output helpers and the dispatch.\n- [ ] Confirm the task is inside this packet's scope: one file, one additive flag.\n- [ ] Re-run the baseline command when a task names one, and compare against `scratch/baseline/`.\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| TASK-SEQ | Complete tasks in order: T004 before T005 and T006; T009 to T012 only after implementation lands |\n| TASK-SCOPE | Only `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` may be modified; packet docs live under `specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/` |\n| CONTRACT | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical; with `--quiet`, stdout is exactly one line and every diagnostic stays on stderr |\n| NO-ALIAS | Do not add `-q`; the request names `--quiet` only |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\nAfter each task: `Task T### complete. Files modified: [list]. Verify: [command output]`\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\nIf blocked: stop, mark the task `[B]`, record the blocker and the failing command output beside it, and escalate — do not widen scope to work around it.\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":91,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":86}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_5a850e51a4bf40dc8426d557d35e30de","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh emits a multi-line human report or a full JSON document, so a caller that needs only the level must parse prose. A --quiet flag prints exactly the final recommended-level line and leaves every existing invocation unchanged.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` answers one question — which SpecKit level a task needs — but its default output is a report of sixteen to eighteen lines, and its `--json` mode is a full document. A caller that wants only the level must therefore grep a sentence out of prose, which breaks silently when the wording moves. This change adds a `--quiet` flag that prints the single `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` line, and nothing else. The proof is a line count: `--quiet` prints exactly one line, and every non-quiet invocation stays byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` selects a shorter stdout path for the text report only; when `--json` is also passed, the JSON document wins. The quiet line and the report's level line are emitted from one shared format site, so the two cannot drift apart.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: None — one bash script, no new tools, no external services.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring` (scaffolded with `--skip-branch`; no dedicated feature branch) |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` prints a human-readable report of sixteen to eighteen lines, or a full JSON document under `--json`, and offers no minimal mode. Tooling that runs the script only to learn the level must capture the report and grep the `Recommended Level:` sentence out of it, which couples the caller to the report's layout and fails silently if that layout changes.\n\n### Purpose\nWith `--quiet`, the script prints exactly its final recommended-level line and nothing else, so a caller reads the level without parsing; without the flag, every existing invocation keeps the output it has today.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--quiet` boolean flag to the argument parser of `recommend-level.sh`.\n- Add a quiet output path that prints only the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` line.\n- Emit the report's level line and the quiet line from one shared format site so the two cannot diverge.\n- Document `--quiet` in the script's header OPTIONS comment and in `show_help()`.\n- Preserve existing behavior exactly: the default report, the `--json` document, error text, stderr routing and exit codes.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- A `-q` short alias — the request names `--quiet` only, and an alias would widen the public interface without a caller asking for it.\n- A bare-value mode that prints `3` alone — it would create a second format for the same fact and break parsers written against today's label.\n- Changes to scoring, phase logic, report wording or the JSON schema — the flag must not alter an existing output contract.\n- An automated test harness for the script — none exists today, so verification is the command matrix in `tasks.md`; adding a harness is its own packet.\n- Edits to any other script or caller — the change is confined to one file.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add a `QUIET` global, a `--quiet` parse case, a shared level-line helper, a quiet dispatch branch, and the help text |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc <n> --files <n>` prints exactly one stdout line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and exits 0. |\n| REQ-002 | With no `--quiet`, stdout is byte-for-byte identical to today's output for every existing invocation, including `--json`, `--no-recommend-phases` and `--help`. |\n| REQ-003 | The quiet line and the report's level line are emitted from one shared format site, so a change to one cannot leave the other behind. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet --json` prints the unchanged JSON document; when both flags are present, `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`. |\n| REQ-005 | Error paths are unchanged: missing `--loc`/`--files`, non-numeric values and unknown options still exit 1 with the same message on stderr, even when `--quiet` is present. |\n| REQ-006 | stderr behavior is unchanged under `--quiet`; the flag suppresses report lines on stdout only. |\n| REQ-007 | `--quiet` appears in the header OPTIONS comment and in `show_help()` output. |\n| REQ-008 | `--quiet` is order-independent and composes with `--recommend-phases`, `--no-recommend-phases` and `--phase-threshold` without error. |\n| REQ-009 | Exit codes are unchanged: 0 on success, 1 on any usage or input error. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db | wc -l` prints `1`, the line reads `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)`, and the exit code is 0.\n- **SC-002**: The default output for a fixed invocation is byte-identical before and after the change — the `diff` of the two captures is empty.\n- **SC-003**: `--quiet --json` output equals `--json` output for the same inputs — the `diff` of the two captures is empty.\n\n**Given** the script runs with `--quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout is exactly the single line `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and the exit code is 0.\n\n**Given** the same invocation without `--quiet`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout equals the pre-change multi-line report byte-for-byte.\n\n**Given** `--quiet --json --loc 100 --files 3`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout is the same JSON document `--json` alone prints.\n\n**Given** `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files`, **When** it runs, **Then** it exits 1, the missing-input error goes to stderr, and stdout is empty.\n\n**Given** `--quiet --bogus`, **When** it runs, **Then** it exits 1 with the unknown-option error on stderr.\n\n**Given** `--help` with or without `--quiet`, **When** it runs, **Then** the help text names `--quiet` and exits 0.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` and coreutils already required by the script | None — no new dependency | The flag is pure bash; nothing is installed |\n| Dependency | Downstream readers of the `Recommended Level:` label | A renamed label breaks them | Reuse the exact existing string through one shared format site and pin it with a byte comparison in verification |\n| Risk | The quiet line and the report line drift apart in a later edit | Med | Both call one helper, so there is a single literal to change; verification diffs the quiet line against the report's line |\n| Risk | `--quiet` hides phase guidance a caller still needs | Low | The flag is opt-in; the default report and `--json` remain for richer reads |\n| Risk | An argument-parser edit regresses an existing flag | Med | Byte-diff the default and JSON outputs against a captured baseline, and run every existing flag in the matrix |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable runtime — the scoring path is unchanged and only the output selection differs; the script still completes in the same tens of milliseconds.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: `--quiet` takes no value, so it adds no input-parsing or injection surface; the script performs no authentication and reads no credential.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: The exit-code contract is unchanged: 0 on success, 1 on any usage or input error, with errors on stderr only.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` alone still fails the required-input check (`--loc` or `--files`), exiting 1 with the error on stderr and nothing on stdout.\n- Maximum input: extreme values such as `--loc 100000 --files 999 --quiet` still print the same single line; there is no buffering or truncation path.\n- Flag position: `--quiet` works anywhere in the argument list, matching the script's existing order-independent parse loop.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A — the script is fully offline and calls no service.\n- Network timeout: N/A — the script makes no network request.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A — the script is single-shot and keeps no intermediate state.\n- Session expiry: N/A — the script has no session concept.\n- Output-channel separation: errors never reach stdout, so a caller capturing stdout under `--quiet` cannot capture an error line by accident.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~15, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 2/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N — additive flag, default output preserved |\n| Research | 2/20 | The script was read end-to-end; no investigation remains |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 1 (readers of the level label) |\n| **Total** | **10/100** | **Level 3** (declared by the authoring dispatch; measured complexity is low, so the packet keeps its scaffolded Level 3 contract) |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The quiet line and the report's level line diverge in a later edit | M | M | One shared format site; verification diffs the quiet output against the report's level line |\n| R-002 | `--quiet` hides phase guidance a caller needs | L | L | Opt-in flag; the default report and `--json` remain for richer reads |\n| R-003 | An argument-parser edit regresses an existing flag | M | L | Byte-diff the default and JSON outputs against the captured baseline; run the full flag matrix |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Scripted caller reads the level in one line (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** dispatch harness, **I want** a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line, **so that** I can capture the level directly instead of grepping a multi-line report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Human keeps the familiar report (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** human operator, **I want** the default output to stay exactly as it is, **so that** the report I read today is unchanged by the new flag.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: JSON consumer keeps a stable document (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** scripted `--json` caller, **I want** JSON output to be unaffected when `--quiet` is also passed, **so that** my schema assumptions hold for every flag combination.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does `--quiet` change `--json` output? No — JSON is already a single machine-readable document, so it wins when both flags are present.\n- Does `--quiet` suppress stderr or skip phase scoring? No — stderr is unchanged and scoring still runs; the flag only selects a shorter stdout path.\n- Does the quiet line keep the `Recommended Level:` label instead of a bare number? Yes — the label is the existing contract, and a bare value would be a second format to maintain.\n- None blocking.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: ADR-001 in `plan.md` §L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n---\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh emits a multi-line human report or a full JSON document, so a caller that needs only the level must parse prose. A --quiet flag prints exactly the final recommended-level line and leaves every existing invocation unchanged.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: