{"toolCallId":"call_70f9139c17ae44128e60d7ef12f8f78b","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Write three sentences: the problem, the change and the proof. GOOD: Callers parse a 17-line report to read one value. The flag prints one line. A line count proves it. WEAK: This document describes the feature. -->\n\n[2-3 sentence high-level overview for stakeholders who need quick context]\n\n**Key Decisions**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 1], [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 2]\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Blocking dependency]\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/recommend-level-quiet`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh emits a multi-line human report or a full JSON document, so a caller that needs only the level must parse prose. A --quiet flag prints exactly the final recommended-level line and leaves every existing invocation unchanged.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` answers one question — which SpecKit level a task needs — but its default output is a report of roughly fifteen lines, and its `--json` mode is a full document. A caller that wants only the level must therefore grep a sentence out of prose, which breaks silently when the wording moves. This change adds a `--quiet` flag that prints the single `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` line, and nothing else. The proof is a line count: `--quiet` prints exactly one line, and every non-quiet invocation stays byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` selects a shorter stdout path for the text report only; when `--json` is also passed, the JSON document wins. The quiet line and the report's level line are emitted from one shared format site, so the two cannot drift apart.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: None — one bash script, no new tools, no external services.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring` (scaffolded with `--skip-branch`; no dedicated feature branch) |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` prints a human-readable report of roughly fifteen lines, or a full JSON document under `--json`, and offers no minimal mode. Tooling that runs the script only to learn the level must capture the report and grep the `Recommended Level:` sentence out of it, which couples the caller to the report's layout and fails silently if that layout changes.\n\n### Purpose\nWith `--quiet`, the script prints exactly its final recommended-level line and nothing else, so a caller reads the level without parsing; without the flag, every existing invocation keeps the output it has today.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--quiet` boolean flag to the argument parser of `recommend-level.sh`.\n- Add a quiet output path that prints only the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` line.\n- Emit the report's level line and the quiet line from one shared format site so the two cannot diverge.\n- Document `--quiet` in the script's header OPTIONS comment and in `show_help()`.\n- Preserve existing behavior exactly: the default report, the `--json` document, error text, stderr routing and exit codes.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- A `-q` short alias — the request names `--quiet` only, and an alias would widen the public interface without a caller asking for it.\n- A bare-value mode that prints `3` alone — it would create a second format for the same fact and break parsers written against today's label.\n- Changes to scoring, phase logic, report wording or the JSON schema — the flag must not alter an existing output contract.\n- An automated test harness for the script — none exists today, so verification is the command matrix in `tasks.md`; adding a harness is its own packet.\n- Edits to any other script or caller — the change is confined to one file.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add a `QUIET` global, a `--quiet` parse case, a shared level-line helper, a quiet dispatch branch, and the help text |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc <n> --files <n>` prints exactly one stdout line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and exits 0. |\n| REQ-002 | With no `--quiet`, stdout is byte-for-byte identical to today's output for every existing invocation, including `--json`, `--no-recommend-phases` and `--help`. |\n| REQ-003 | The quiet line and the report's level line are emitted from one shared format site, so a change to one cannot leave the other behind. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet --json` prints the unchanged JSON document; when both flags are present, `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`. |\n| REQ-005 | Error paths are unchanged: missing `--loc`/`--files`, non-numeric values and unknown options still exit 1 with the same message on stderr, even when `--quiet` is present. |\n| REQ-006 | stderr behavior is unchanged under `--quiet`; the flag suppresses report lines on stdout only. |\n| REQ-007 | `--quiet` appears in the header OPTIONS comment and in `show_help()` output. |\n| REQ-008 | `--quiet` is order-independent and composes with `--recommend-phases`, `--no-recommend-phases` and `--phase-threshold` without error. |\n| REQ-009 | Exit codes are unchanged: 0 on success, 1 on any usage or input error. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db | wc -l` prints `1`, the line reads `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)`, and the exit code is 0.\n- **SC-002**: The default output for a fixed invocation is byte-identical before and after the change — the `diff` of the two captures is empty.\n- **SC-003**: `--quiet --json` output equals `--json` output for the same inputs — the `diff` of the two captures is empty.\n\n**Given** the script runs with `--quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout is exactly the single line `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and the exit code is 0.\n\n**Given** the same invocation without `--quiet`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout equals the pre-change multi-line report byte-for-byte.\n\n**Given** `--quiet --json --loc 100 --files 3`, **When** it exits, **Then** stdout is the same JSON document `--json` alone prints.\n\n**Given** `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files`, **When** it runs, **Then** it exits 1, the missing-input error goes to stderr, and stdout is empty.\n\n**Given** `--quiet --bogus`, **When** it runs, **Then** it exits 1 with the unknown-option error on stderr.\n\n**Given** `--help` with or without `--quiet`, **When** it runs, **Then** the help text names `--quiet` and exits 0.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` and coreutils already required by the script | None — no new dependency | The flag is pure bash; nothing is installed |\n| Dependency | Downstream readers of the `Recommended Level:` label | A renamed label breaks them | Reuse the exact existing string through one shared format site and pin it with a byte comparison in verification |\n| Risk | The quiet line and the report line drift apart in a later edit | Med | Both call one helper, so there is a single literal to change; verification diffs the quiet line against the report's line |\n| Risk | `--quiet` hides phase guidance a caller still needs | Low | The flag is opt-in; the default report and `--json` remain for richer reads |\n| Risk | An argument-parser edit regresses an existing flag | Med | Byte-diff the default and JSON outputs against a captured baseline, and run every existing flag in the matrix |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable runtime — the scoring path is unchanged and only the output selection differs; the script still completes in the same tens of milliseconds.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: `--quiet` takes no value, so it adds no input-parsing or injection surface; the script performs no authentication and reads no credential.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: The exit-code contract is unchanged: 0 on success, 1 on any usage or input error, with errors on stderr only.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` alone still fails the required-input check (`--loc` or `--files`), exiting 1 with the error on stderr and nothing on stdout.\n- Maximum input: extreme values such as `--loc 100000 --files 999 --quiet` still print the same single line; there is no buffering or truncation path.\n- Flag position: `--quiet` works anywhere in the argument list, matching the script's existing order-independent parse loop.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A — the script is fully offline and calls no service.\n- Network timeout: N/A — the script makes no network request.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A — the script is single-shot and keeps no intermediate state.\n- Session expiry: N/A — the script has no session concept.\n- Output-channel separation: errors never reach stdout, so a caller capturing stdout under `--quiet` cannot capture an error line by accident.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~15, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 2/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N — additive flag, default output preserved |\n| Research | 2/20 | The script was read end-to-end; no investigation remains |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 1 (readers of the level label) |\n| **Total** | **10/100** | **Level 3** (declared by the authoring dispatch; measured complexity is low, so the packet keeps its scaffolded Level 3 contract) |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The quiet line and the report's level line diverge in a later edit | M | M | One shared format site; verification diffs the quiet output against the report's level line |\n| R-002 | `--quiet` hides phase guidance a caller needs | L | L | Opt-in flag; the default report and `--json` remain for richer reads |\n| R-003 | An argument-parser edit regresses an existing flag | M | L | Byte-diff the default and JSON outputs against the captured baseline; run the full flag matrix |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Scripted caller reads the level in one line (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** dispatch harness, **I want** a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line, **so that** I can capture the level directly instead of grepping a multi-line report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Human keeps the familiar report (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** human operator, **I want** the default output to stay exactly as it is, **so that** the report I read today is unchanged by the new flag.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: JSON consumer keeps a stable document (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** scripted `--json` caller, **I want** JSON output to be unaffected when `--quiet` is also passed, **so that** my schema assumptions hold for every flag combination.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does `--quiet` change `--json` output? No — JSON is already a single machine-readable document, so it wins when both flags are present.\n- Does `--quiet` suppress stderr or skip phase scoring? No — stderr is unchanged and scoring still runs; the flag only selects a shorter stdout path.\n- Does the quiet line keep the `Recommended Level:` label instead of a bare number? Yes — the label is the existing contract, and a bare value would be a second format to maintain.\n- None blocking.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: ADR-001 in `plan.md` §L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n---\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":85,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":58}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_738fc888a69445e9b9ec36ca6c4d11ba","title":"Ran bash, python3","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"SPECKIT_JSON=true bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag --strict 2>/dev/null > /tmp/v.json; python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('/tmp/v.json'))\nprint(list(d.keys()))\nfor k,v in d.items():\n    if isinstance(v,list):\n        print(k,'list',len(v))\n        for item in v:\n            s=json.dumps(item)[:220]\n            if 'AC_' in s or 'CLOSURE' in s.upper(): print('  ',s)\n\"","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"SPECKIT_JSON=true bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag --strict 2>/dev/null > /tmp/v.json; python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('/tmp/v.json'))\nprint(list(d.keys()))\nfor k,v in d.items():\n    if isinstance(v,list):\n        print(k,'list',len(v))\n        for item in v:\n            s=json.dumps(item)[:220]\n            if 'AC_' in s or 'CLOSURE' in s.upper(): print('  ',s)\n\"","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/217-weak-model-spec-authoring"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["bash","python3"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
